Z||000000||Philphine||07-24-2001||12:11 PM||||<BR>something the wing thread got me thinking about. i want to put one on my chop top project. i was just kind of measuring the one on my coupe to see how much one would need to be cut down to peek over the roof like normal. but i got to thinking, what if i didn't cut it down. it would sit about 3" over the roof. would that increase the chance of getting a ram air effect if i made it functional (i want to).<P> i sat my coupe one on it and it looks kind of funny sitting that high but if there were some practical gains from it it might be worth doing (at least half of the people who see it on a normal coupe think it looks strange anyway). i guess i also have the option of cutting it down to something in bettween. say, just high enough that if a sunroof were open it would just be higher than the trailing edge of the glass.<P>just thought i'd try to get some opinions on it.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>SERENITY NOW!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||LZeitgeist||07-24-2001||01:07 PM||||Since the roofline would be sitting 3" lower than normal, why not increase the size of the intake on the scoop by 3", while leaving the overall scoop height the same? (Put some sort of grille over the opening so you don't suck birds into your engine while you're at it...)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Johnny07||07-24-2001||01:18 PM||||There is a benefit to having the scoop up higher due to the turbulence coming off the car. How much? I am not sure but use the early 70's firebird formulas and some of the Chrysler cars from the late 60's and early 70's as examples of how high they came up. The raised the opening up a specific height to get clean airflow but I can't find the info right now. I will look for it tonight and back this up with the numbers||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||07-24-2001||01:40 PM||||you might try driving at highway speeds with the sunroof out, and stick your hand up into the airflow and see where it maxes out.<P>  there was a nuther whole thread on ram air stuff that also started getting 'opinionated' a while back.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mach10||07-24-2001||02:08 PM||||I think the biggest problem would only be that you're ramming air DOWN where it wants to come UP. Unless, of course, you're plumbing the thing only to the air intake... <BR>Aesthetically, I never really liked them. Take my advice, and don't listen to me. Do what you think looks cool. You'll catch flack no matter what you do (occasionally just on the basis of simply OWNING a Fiero  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ), so you might as well make it look good to you. Far as I can tell, there's a 50/50 split between people who love or hate the over-the-roof scoop things.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Philphine||07-24-2001||07:40 PM||||lz, i kinda thought of that, but i would probaly mess up the scoop tyring to do that. i also have a couple of small naca ducts. i could cut one into the top to try to catch some of the air passing over it and leave it at a normal height in relation to the roof (peeking over).<P>johnny07, thats what i'm thinking but i don't know if there is enought benifit. i'm also wondering if the lower roof line and more slanted windshield would mean it wouldn't need to be any higher anyway.<P>ken, i do that a lot in my coupe, but i can only get my finger tips out. i figure if i take the sunroof all the way out it will have it's own effect on airflow (just opening it dose too i guess) i do notice air rushing on my fingertips though.<P>mach10, yes i want to seal it off if i can so it is all for the intake (i think it's giving me cooling problems on my coupe now, but i don't want to bother with working out a better intake for this engine). as for looks, i get responses both ways for the one on my coupe so it's not too big a deal. i like 'em. i hope to end up with one on my indy too.<P>thanks for the responce. hope there's a few more opinions.||||reg||1
